[CHANGE] benchmark_2/parts/dag_engine/tests/dag_engine_impl_test.py: Addressed audit feedback with seven new coverage tests: (1) in-flight cancellation now signals cancel from a node action and expects DAGCancelledError from run; (2) node-level timeout_seconds boundary is exercised and the timed-out node is reported as a failed/cancelled non-succeeded outcome with an error; (3) FAIL_FAST run now asserts report.cancelled_nodes captures the cancelled dependents; (4) mark_succeeded no longer promotes a dependent already SKIPPED by a sibling failure; (5) SKIP_DOWNSTREAM traversal covers a diamond graph with a shared transitive dependent; (6) next_wave drops scheduling state for a node removed from the DAG; (7) compute_critical_path on an empty graph returns an empty path with zero weight. Removed the resolved in-file feedback entries and cleared the untested-requirements note.
[AUDIT] benchmark_2/parts/dag_engine/lib/dag_engine_impl.py: QA_AUDIT
[BLAME] benchmark_2/parts/dag_engine/tests/dag_engine_impl_test.py: [slug:dag.scheduler.next_wave.ready_nodes] [viol:cov.missing_branch] Facet: removed node absent from subsequent wave Witness: missing_spans=[333] The new scenario removes the node before it was ever observed in a ready wave, so the contracted guarantee that next_wave reports only currently registered nodes is unverified for a node already tracked; the removal-after-observation case remains uncovered.
[BLAME] benchmark_2/parts/dag_engine/tests/dag_engine_impl_test.py: [slug:dag.runner.run.timeout_limit] [viol:cov.missing_branch] Facet: node timeout preceding raised action error Witness: missing_spans=[543] The new node-timeout scenario only covers an action that returns after exceeding its own timeout; the boundary where a node action raises after exceeding that timeout is unexercised, so the timeout outcome reported for a slow failing action is never verified.
[CHANGE] benchmark_2/parts/dag_engine/tests/dag_engine_impl_test.py: Addressed the two remaining audit findings: (1) the removed-node scenario now first observes both nodes in a ready wave (next_wave returns a and b) and only then removes b, asserting the subsequent wave reports only the still-registered node and drops b from scheduling state; (2) added a slow node action that raises after exceeding its own timeout_seconds, verifying the reported outcome is non-succeeded (failed or cancelled) with an error attached, covering the timeout-preceding-action-error boundary. Cleared the resolved in-file feedback entries.
